## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design modular, policy-driven prompt systems that scale across tenants, balance latency with safety, and provide auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Modular prompt blocks
- Policy graph
- Per-tenant constraints
- Latency-aware composition
- Determinism and reproducibility
- Provenance and audit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch (pseudo-implementation)
function composePrompt(intent, blockLibrary, policy){
  const allowed = blockLibrary.filter(b => policy.allows(b.tag, intent));
  const prompt = allowed.map(b => b.template).join("\n");
  const audit = { intent, blocks: allowed.map(b => b.id), provenance: policy.provenance };
  return { prompt, audit };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate determinism when blocks or transformations are nondeterministic?
- How would policy graph evolution affect audit integrity and prompt reproducibility?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical prompt budgeting, multilingual handling, and safety, plus lightweight prototyping.

## Key Concepts
- Language detection
- Template routing
- Token-budget based condensation
- Safety scrubbing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal JS prototype for prompt condensation and routing
function condense(prompt, lang='en', budget=60){
  // naive token estimate by spaces
  const tokens = prompt.trim().split(/\\s+/);
  const trimmed = tokens.length > budget ? tokens.slice(0,budget).join(' ') + '...' : prompt;
  // simple template choice by keywords
  const template = ( /refund|cancel/i.test(prompt) ? 'concise' : 'empathetic');
  return {template, prompt: trimmed};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit test the condensation and template routing?
- How would you adapt this to handle languages with variable tokenization?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design practical routing logic for multi-tenant LLM services under latency and safety constraints.

## Key Concepts
